## Development Process

### Team

This project had four members: Juee, Arushi, Siya, and Sahar.

For the checkpoint submission, Arushi took the lead, formulating the research question and producing three of the five visualizations, while Siya and Sahar each contributed one visualization. Arushi also recorded and submitted the video. Members worked independently and each cleaned the dataset in their own way, though the underlying dataset and research question were shared.

For the final submission, after the research question was formualted, Juee built the interactive D3.js visualization and we all reviewed the write-up.

### Development Process

The most time-consuming part of this project was understanding and accessing the data. CMIP6 is a large, complex climate dataset with its own terminology: experiment IDs, source IDs, table IDs, Zarr stores, SSP scenarios. None of this was familiar at the start. A significant amount of time was spent learning what the catalog columns meant, identifying which model and scenarios had the data needed (GFDL-ESM4 being one of the few models with SSP1-1.9), and resolving authentication issues with the Google Cloud Storage bucket that hosts the data.

Once the data was understood and successfully loaded, processing it in Python (filtering to San Diego's grid cell, computing the pre-industrial baseline, isolating June–August months, applying a rolling average, and exporting a clean CSV) was relatively straightforward.

Building the D3.js visualization took the second-largest share of time. The main challenges were decoupling the brush and zoom interactions so they didn't conflict with each other, implementing the animated line drawing, and ensuring the sidebar toggles correctly updated the crossing dot markers as scenarios were hidden and shown.

In total, between all 4 members the final submission represents approximately **10-15 person-hours** of work, the majority spent on data exploration and the D3 implementation.

